{{smaller block|Of ornaments . . . they [the women of El-Medinah] have a vast variety, . . . and they delight in strong perfumes, — musk, civet, ''ambergris'', attar of rose, oil of jasmine, aloe-wood, and extract of cinnamon.
{{right|''R. F. Burton'', El-Medinah, p. 282.}}}}

{{smaller block|''Ambergris'' is a sort of bezoar, found in the alimentary canal of the cachalot, and seemingly derived from the fatty matter contained in the Cephalopoda upon which the Cetacean feeds. {{right|''Huxley'', Anat. Vert., p. 341.}}}}

'''amber-seed''' (am′bėr-sēd), ''n.'' The seed of ''Hibiscus Abelmoschus'', a plant cultivated in most warm countries. {{smaller|These seeds have a musky odor, and are often used to perfume pomatum. The Arabs mix them with their coffee. Also called ''musk-seed'' and ''ambrette''.}}

'''amber-tree''' (am′bėr-trē), ''n.'' The English name for ''Anthospermum'', a genus of African shrubs with evergreen leaves, which when bruised emit a fragrant odor.

'''ambes-ace†, ambs-ace†''' (āmz′ās), ''n.'' [< ME. ''ambesas'', ''ambezas'', < OF. ''ambesas'', ''ambezas'' (F. ''ambesas''), < ''ambes'' (< L. ''ambo'', both) + ''as'', ace: see ''ambi-'' and ''ace''.] The double ace, the lowest cast at dice; hence, ill luck, misfortune. Also spelled ''ames-ace''.

{{smaller block|Your bagges ben not filled with ''ambes-as''.
{{right|''Chaucer'', Man of Law’s Tale, l. 26.}}}}

{{smaller block|I had rather be in this choice than throw ''ames-ace'' for my life. {{right|''Shak.'', All’s Well, ii. 3.}}}}

{{smaller block|Æschylus, it seems to me, is willing, just as Shakspere is, to risk the prosperity of a verse upon a lucky throw of words, which may come up the sices of hardy metaphor or the ''ambsace'' of conceit.}}

