{{smaller block|Ignorant I was of the human frame, and of its latent powers, as regarded speed, force, and ''ambidexterity''.
{{right|''De Quincey''.}}}}
'''2.''' Double-dealing; duplicity.
{{smaller block|That intricate net of general misery, spun out of his own crafty ''ambidexterity''.
{{right|''I. D’Israeli'', Amen. of Lit., I. 412.}}}}
'''3.''' In ''law'', the taking of money by a juror from both parties for a verdict.

'''ambidextral''' (am-bi-deks′tra̤l), ''a.'' [< ''ambidexter'' + ''-al''.] Placed on either side of a given thing indifferently: as, “the ''ambidextral'' adjective,” ''Earle''. [Rare.]

'''ambidextrous''' (am-bi-deks′trus), ''a.'' [< ''ambidexter'' + ''-ous'', after ''dexterous''.] '''1.''' Having the faculty of using both hands with equal ease and dexterity; hence, skilful; facile.
{{smaller block|Nature is prolific and ''ambidextrous''.
{{right|''O. W. Holmes'', Old Vol. of Life, p. 420.}}}}
'''2.''' Practising or siding with both parties; double-dealing; deceitful.
{{smaller block|Shuffling and ''ambidextrous'' dealings.
{{right|''Sir R. L’Estrange''.}}
Edward Gosynhyll . . . mending his ''ambidextrous'' pen for “The Praise of all Women.”
{{right|''I. D’Israeli'', Amen. of Lit., I. 305.}}}}

