'''ambiguity''' (am-bi-gū′i-ti), ''n.''; pl. ''ambiguities'' (-tiz). [< ME. ''ambiguite'' (rare), < L. ''ambiguita(t-)s'', < ''ambiguus'': see ''ambiguous''.] '''1.''' The state of being ambiguous; doubtfulness or uncertainty, particularly of signification.

{{smaller block|The words are of single meaning without any ''ambiguity''.
{{right|''South.''}}}}

{{smaller block|If we would keep our conclusions free from ''ambiguity'', we must reserve the term we employ to signify absolute rectitude solely for this purpose.
{{right|''H. Spencer'', Social Statics, p. 510.}}}}

'''2.''' An equivocal or ambiguous expression.

{{smaller block|Let our author, therefore, come out of his mists and ''ambiguities'', or give us some better authority for his unreasonable doubts.
{{right|''Dryden'', To Duchess of York.}}}}

'''ambiguous''' (am-big′ū-us), ''a.'' [< L. ''ambiguus'', going about, changeable, doubtful, uncertain, < ''ambigere'', go about, wander, doubt, < ''ambi-'', around, + ''agere'', drive, move: see ''agent''.] '''1.''' Of doubtful or uncertain nature; wanting clearness or definiteness; difficult to comprehend or distinguish; indistinct; obscure.

{{smaller block|Even the most dextrous distances of the old masters . . . are ''ambiguous''.
{{right|''Ruskin'', Mod. Painters, I. ii. 2.}}}}

{{smaller block|Stratified rocks of ''ambiguous'' character.
{{right|''Murchison'', Silur. Syst., p. 418. (''N. E. D.'')}}}}

'''2.''' Of doubtful purport; open to various interpretations; having a double meaning; equivocal.

{{smaller block|<poem>What have been thy answers, what but dark,
''Ambiguous'', and with double sense deluding?</poem>
{{right|''Milton'', P. R., i. 435.}}}}

{{smaller block|He was recalled by the Duchess, whose letters had been uniformly so ''ambiguous'' that he confessed he was quite unable to divine their meaning.
{{right|''Motley'', Dutch Republic, II. 23.}}}}

readings.c3s4:
¶
'''3.''' Wavering; undecided; hesitating: as, “''ambiguous'' in all their doings,” ''Milton'', Eikonoklastes (1649), p. 239. [Rare or obsolete.]
{{smaller block|<poem>Th’ ''ambiguous'' god, who ruled her lab’ring breast,
In these mysterious words his mind exprest. ''Dryden.''</poem>}}

'''4.''' Using obscure or equivocal language.
{{smaller block|What mutterest thou with thine ''ambiguous'' mouth?
{{right|''Swinburne'', Atalanta, l. 1500.}}}}
{{smaller block|='''Syn. 2.''' ''Equivocal'', etc. (see ''obscure''), indeterminate, indefinite, indistinct, not clear, not plain, amphibolous, dubious, vague, enigmatical, dark, blind.}}

'''ambiguously''' (am-big′ụ̄-us-li), ''adv.'' In an ambiguous manner; with doubtful meaning.
{{smaller block|<poem>Why play . . . into the devil’s hands
By dealing so ''ambiguously?''</poem>
{{right|''Browning'', Ring and Book, I. 321.}}}}

'''ambiguousness''' (am-big′ụ̄-us-nes), ''n.'' The quality of being ambiguous; ambiguity; obscurity.

'''ambilevous†''' (am-bi-lē′vus), ''a.'' [< L. ''ambi-'', on both sides, + ''lævus'' (= Gr. λαιός, for *λαιϝός), left. Cf. ''ambidexter''.] Unable to use either hand with facility: the opposite of ''ambidextrous''. [Rare.]
{{smaller block|Some are as Galen hath expressed; that is, ''ambilevous'', or left-handed on both sides; such as with agility and vigour have not the use of either.
{{right|''Sir T. Browne'', Vulg. Err., p. 189.}}}}
